WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.221
A first-order approximation uses the function value and gradient at a centre. A second-order approximation also

00:00:07.221 --> 00:00:14.242
uses curvature. In one dimension curvature is a second derivative; with several coordinates it becomes the

00:00:14.242 --> 00:00:15.120
Hessian matrix.

00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:21.825
Taylor expansion describes how the function changes around a named centre. The displacement h is not

00:00:21.825 --> 00:00:23.373
the new input itself.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:28.480
The derivative gives the tangent term. This approximation ignores curvature.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.493
If h is halved, how will the first-order error change for this quadratic? Pause here and

00:00:34.493 --> 00:00:36.210
make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:45.225
In one dimension the Hessian is the second derivative. The quadratic term measures bending away from

00:00:45.225 --> 00:00:52.623
the tangent. For our starting inputs, Linear prediction: 6. Curvature correction: 0.25. Exact value: 6.25.

00:00:53.133 --> 00:01:01.063
For this degree-two polynomial the second-order expansion is exact. More general functions have higher-order remainder terms.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:08.067
Choose a small, stated neighbourhood before trusting a Taylor approximation or a finite-difference gradient check.

